WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:03.870
Last year I filmed on a railway in Scotland
called the West Highland Line.

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:07.674
And as part of that video,
I got to travel on a “road-rail vehicle”,

00:00:07.674 --> 00:00:10.710
basically a van which also has a set
of retractable train wheels.

00:00:10.710 --> 00:00:13.613
For the people who work on the railway,
it's everyday mundane stuff,

00:00:13.613 --> 00:00:16.950
but it's a delight for this sort of nerd.

00:00:16.950 --> 00:00:18.451
But riding on one of those isn't

00:00:18.451 --> 00:00:20.887
generally the sort of thing
that the public can do...

00:00:20.887 --> 00:00:25.025
except here, on the coast of Tokushima
Prefecture in the south-west of Japan.

00:00:25.025 --> 00:00:26.526
It took a long while to get here.

00:00:26.526 --> 00:00:30.764
There was a lot of travel along beautiful
coastline, but now I can show you this:

00:00:30.764 --> 00:00:34.300
what the Asa Coast Railway
calls the “DMV”, dual mode vehicle.

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:36.102
It's a bus and it's a train.

00:00:36.102 --> 00:00:40.006
And it takes about 15 seconds
to switch from one to the other.

00:00:40.006 --> 00:00:40.840
[in Japanese]

00:00:56.856 --> 00:00:58.691
So right now, this is just a regular bus.

00:00:58.691 --> 00:01:00.026
And there are plenty of places
in the world

00:01:00.026 --> 00:01:02.829
who've come up with solutions
to making buses go faster.

00:01:02.829 --> 00:01:04.964
Separate bus lanes, guided busways.

00:01:04.964 --> 00:01:08.334
So why this, and why here?

00:02:14.134 --> 00:02:17.170
[recorded announcement]
“The DMV will now change to train mode.”

00:02:17.170 --> 00:02:20.473
“Mōdochenji, sutāto!”

00:02:20.473 --> 00:02:21.808
[child’s voice]
“Hai!”

00:02:21.808 --> 00:02:23.209
[recorded drumming]

00:02:28.515 --> 00:02:30.316
[drumming continues,
children chant]

00:02:42.729 --> 00:02:45.498
[recorded track ends]
“Finish!”

00:03:48.561 --> 00:03:51.030
The folks here are clear that
this is partly a tourist attraction.

00:03:51.030 --> 00:03:53.866
They've got a gift shop
with plush versions of the vehicle.

00:03:53.866 --> 00:03:56.236
You can buy “dual mode curry”,

00:03:56.236 --> 00:03:58.972
and they've got rock candy
printed with the design.

00:03:58.972 --> 00:04:01.374
Yes, it would be more practical
to have a small train

00:04:01.374 --> 00:04:04.277
and ask people to change onto a bus,
the same as basically everywhere else.

00:04:04.277 --> 00:04:06.679
They could have ripped up the rails
and had a guided busway.

00:04:06.679 --> 00:04:09.716
Or they could have just used
a bus on the road!

00:04:09.716 --> 00:04:11.384
There aren't too many
traffic jams out here.

00:04:11.384 --> 00:04:14.487
And even here, the bus is
still powered by the rear tires.

00:04:14.487 --> 00:04:16.889
The metal train wheels
are just for guidance.

00:04:16.889 --> 00:04:20.493
But it's not just about “what is the
most practical or the cheapest solution”.

00:04:20.493 --> 00:04:24.230
It's about positioning the Asa Coast Railway
in this region long term,

00:04:24.230 --> 00:04:27.233
attracting the sort of tourists
who want to ride on a

00:04:27.233 --> 00:04:29.669
road-rail vehicle.

